[Term]
id: VBO:0008044
name: CROATIAN SHEPHERD, Croatia (Dog)
comment: The concept of “breed reported in geographic location by FAO National Coordinators” is unique to DAD-IS (https://www.fao.org/dad-is/en/). This VBO term should therefore be used specifically for the breed conforming to this concept.
synonym: "CROATIAN SHEPHERD" EXACT most_common_name [] {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"}
synonym: "HRVATSKI OVČAR" EXACT [] {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"}
is_a: NCBITaxon:9615 {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"} ! Canis lupus familiaris
is_a: VBO:0400000 {source="https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4142-7153", source="https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5002-8648"} ! Vertebrate breed
relationship: VBO:0300020 http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q224 {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"} ! breed reported in geographic location
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4142-7153
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5520-6597
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9178-3965
property_value: dcterms:source "https://www.fao.org/dad-is" xsd:anyURI
property_value: origindescription "This breed was most probably developed over several centuries out of the descendants of the so-called √¢‚Ç¨≈ìPfahlbauhund√¢‚Ç¨¬ù (Canis Familiaris Palustris) in the area of eastern Slavonic, which is a part of the Republic of Croatia. According to the hypothesis in the manuscript of Bishop Petar Bakic in the year 1719, which again refers to an earlier manuscript of the Bishop Petar of the year 1374, immigrants brought this breed into the homeland. Prof. Stjepan Romic started the systematic breeding of the Croatian Shepherd Dog in the year 1935. The breed was first publicly presented at the first state dog show on 29th and 30th of October 1949 in Zagreb. Dr. Otto Rohr wrote the first breed standard in the year 1951. The standard was published by the FCI on 31st of January 1969." xsd:string {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"}

[Term]
id: VBO:0008045
name: DALMATIAN, Croatia (Dog)
comment: The concept of “breed reported in geographic location by FAO National Coordinators” is unique to DAD-IS (https://www.fao.org/dad-is/en/). This VBO term should therefore be used specifically for the breed conforming to this concept.
synonym: "DALMATIAN" EXACT most_common_name [] {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"}
synonym: "DALMATINSKI PAS" EXACT [] {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"}
is_a: NCBITaxon:9615 {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"} ! Canis lupus familiaris
is_a: VBO:0400000 {source="https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4142-7153", source="https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5002-8648"} ! Vertebrate breed
relationship: VBO:0300020 http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q224 {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"} ! breed reported in geographic location
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4142-7153
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5520-6597
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9178-3965
property_value: dcterms:source "https://www.fao.org/dad-is" xsd:anyURI
property_value: hasdomesticationstatus VBO:0300007 {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"}
property_value: origindescription "The origin of the Dalmatian dog can be recognized in paintings and in church chronicles from the 16th to the 18th century. Dalmatians can be found on the altar painting √¢‚Ç¨≈ìMadonna with Jesus and Angels√¢‚Ç¨¬ù in the church √¢‚Ç¨≈ìGospa od andjela√¢‚Ç¨¬ù in the town Veli Losinj, island Losinj in Croatia dating back to 1600 √¢‚Ç¨‚Äú 1630 and also in a fresco in Zaostrog, Dalmatia, Croatia. It suggests that the Dalmatian originates from the eastern Mediterranean region, in particular the historic province Dalmatia. The first descriptions of the Dalmatian were found in the diocese Djakovo, in the Croatia, namely in the church chronicles of the Bishop Petar Bakic from the year 1719 and the church chronicles of Andreas Keczkemety from the year 1737. The dog was named with the Latin name √¢‚Ç¨≈ìCanis Dalmaticus√¢‚Ç¨¬ù and the height of the dog described with 4-5 √¢‚Ç¨≈ìSpithamus√¢‚Ç¨¬ù. Thomas Pennant described this breed in his work √¢‚Ç¨≈ìSynopsis of Quadrupeds√¢‚Ç¨¬ù in the year 1771 as very independent, named it √¢‚Ç¨≈ìDalmatian√¢‚Ç¨¬ù and wrote the origin of this breed to be Dalmatia. A work by Thomas Bewick, published in 1790, refers to this breed as √¢‚Ç¨≈ìDalmatian or Coach Dog√¢‚Ç¨¬ù. The first unofficial Dalmatian Standard was written by an Englishman named Vero Shaw in the year 1882. After the formation of the Dalmatian Club in England in the year 1890 this standard was transferred to the first official breed standard. The FCI published the first Dalmatian standard on the 7th of April 1955 under the name of √¢‚Ç¨≈ìDalmatian Huntingdog√¢‚Ç¨¬ù." xsd:string {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"}

[Term]
id: VBO:0008046
name: ISTRIAN SHORT-HAIRED HOUND, Croatia (Dog)
comment: The concept of “breed reported in geographic location by FAO National Coordinators” is unique to DAD-IS (https://www.fao.org/dad-is/en/). This VBO term should therefore be used specifically for the breed conforming to this concept.
synonym: "ISTRASKI GONIČ KRATKE DLAKE" EXACT [] {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"}
synonym: "ISTRIAN SHORT-HAIRED HOUND" EXACT most_common_name [] {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"}
is_a: NCBITaxon:9615 {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"} ! Canis lupus familiaris
is_a: VBO:0400000 {source="https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4142-7153", source="https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5002-8648"} ! Vertebrate breed
relationship: VBO:0300020 http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q224 {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"} ! breed reported in geographic location
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4142-7153
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5520-6597
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9178-3965
property_value: dcterms:source "https://www.fao.org/dad-is" xsd:anyURI
property_value: origindescription "The breed descents from an old type of the √¢‚Ç¨≈ìEast Adriatic white hound with markings√¢‚Ç¨¬ù. The earliest proof of this can be seen in the Franciscan monastery in Dubrovnik, which originates from the time between 1327 and 1348. The Capital column of four dogs shows the old type of this hound. The Istrian Short Haired Hound can be found on a painting from the year 1474 √¢‚Ç¨≈ìThe bow of the three kings√¢‚Ç¨¬ù in the cemetery chapel next to the village of Beram (Istrien). The altar painting from the year 1476 √¢‚Ç¨≈ìThe mother of Mercifulness√¢‚Ç¨¬ù in the church of the holy Francis in Zadar, where in the left bottom corner an ancestor of today√¢‚Ç¨‚Ñ¢s Istrian Hound is shown. He was shown then with a three-coloured coat. The description of this hound can be found in the manuscript of the Dakovoe Bishop Petar Bakic from the year 1719, where it says that the breeding of the Istrian Hound was already known before the 14th century. The first Standard for the Short-haired Istrian Hound was published by the FCI on the 2nd of April 1955." xsd:string {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"}

[Term]
id: VBO:0008047
name: ISTRIAN WIRE-HAIRED HOUND, Croatia (Dog)
comment: The concept of “breed reported in geographic location by FAO National Coordinators” is unique to DAD-IS (https://www.fao.org/dad-is/en/). This VBO term should therefore be used specifically for the breed conforming to this concept.
synonym: "ISTARSKI GONIČ OÅ TRE DLAKE" EXACT [] {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"}
synonym: "ISTRIAN WIRE-HAIRED HOUND" EXACT most_common_name [] {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"}
is_a: NCBITaxon:9615 {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"} ! Canis lupus familiaris
is_a: VBO:0400000 {source="https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4142-7153", source="https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5002-8648"} ! Vertebrate breed
relationship: VBO:0300020 http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q224 {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"} ! breed reported in geographic location
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4142-7153
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5520-6597
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9178-3965
property_value: dcterms:source "https://www.fao.org/dad-is" xsd:anyURI
property_value: origindescription "The breed descends from the √¢‚Ç¨≈ìEast Adriatic white hound with markings√¢‚Ç¨¬ù. The hair is wire-haired due to the climate, where the breed was bred. These are mountain areas at the east Adriatic coast, where temperatures are low. The earliest description of the wire-haired Istrian Hound can be found in the manuscript of the Dakovoe Bishop Petar Bakic from the year 1719, where it says that the breeding of this breed was already known before the 14th century. The earliest picture document of this breed can be found in the cathedral of the Evangelist Markus in Makarska. The FCI published the first standard for the Istrian WireHaired Hound on the 6th of April 1955." xsd:string {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"}

[Term]
id: VBO:0008048
name: POSAVATZ HOUND, Croatia (Dog)
comment: The concept of “breed reported in geographic location by FAO National Coordinators” is unique to DAD-IS (https://www.fao.org/dad-is/en/). This VBO term should therefore be used specifically for the breed conforming to this concept.
synonym: "POSAVATZ HOUND" EXACT most_common_name [] {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"}
synonym: "POSAVSKI GONIČ" EXACT [] {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"}
is_a: NCBITaxon:9615 {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"} ! Canis lupus familiaris
is_a: VBO:0400000 {source="https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4142-7153", source="https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5002-8648"} ! Vertebrate breed
relationship: VBO:0300020 http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q224 {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"} ! breed reported in geographic location
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4142-7153
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5520-6597
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9178-3965
property_value: dcterms:source "https://www.fao.org/dad-is" xsd:anyURI
property_value: hasdomesticationstatus VBO:0300007 {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"}
property_value: origindescription "Posavatz, the scent hound from the Save Valley is an old hound breed, which descents from a type of the √¢‚Ç¨≈ìIllyrischen Hound of Red Colour with markings√¢‚Ç¨¬ù. In the cemetery chapel next to the village of Beram (Istrien) one can find in the fresco, dating from 1474, √¢‚Ç¨≈ìThe bow of the three Kings√¢‚Ç¨¬ù a dark wheaten dog with dropped ears. This was the first portrayal of the Posavatz Hound. The next portrayal is the altar fresco √¢‚Ç¨≈ìThe motherhood of the Holy Virgin Mary√¢‚Ç¨¬ù from the 16th century in the Mother of the Angels Church in Veli Losinj, where the ancestor of today√¢‚Ç¨‚Ñ¢s Posavatz Hound is depicted. There is also a description of this hound in a manuscript from the year 1719 of the Dakovear Bishop Petar Bakic, where it says, that the breeding of these hounds was already well known in the 14th century. Franjo Bertic describes the Posavatz Hound in the year 1854. The FCI published the first Standard for the Posavatz Hound on April 8th 1955." xsd:string {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"}

