is_a: VBO:0001093 {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"} ! West African Dongola (Horse)
is_a: VBO:0400000 {source="https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4142-7153", source="https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5002-8648"} ! Vertebrate breed
relationship: VBO:0300020 http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q929 {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"} ! breed reported in geographic location
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4142-7153
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5520-6597
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9178-3965
property_value: dcterms:source "https://www.fao.org/dad-is" xsd:anyURI
property_value: hasdomesticationstatus VBO:0300007 {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"}
property_value: origindescription "descended from Dongola (Sudan): Northern Sudan used to be in permanent contact with the Muslim population of lake Tchad area and north-east Nigeria. It also happened that Sudanese soldiers occupied lake Tchad countries shortly before and during the Mahdia reign. Furthermore, pilgrims from eastern countries of West Africa going to Mekka passed through Sudan and nomadic Fulani, well known as brave warriors and fond of horses, regularly moved between Sudan and eastern West Africa. All these factors were responsible for the propagation of Dongola horses in eastern West African countries." xsd:string {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"}

