[Term]
id: VBO:0011943
name: South African Black Ostrich, South Africa (Ostrich)
comment: The concept of “breed reported in geographic location by FAO National Coordinators” is unique to DAD-IS (https://www.fao.org/dad-is/en/). This VBO term should therefore be used specifically for the breed conforming to this concept.
synonym: "South African Black Ostrich" EXACT most_common_name [] {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"}
is_a: NCBITaxon:8801 {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"} ! Struthio camelus
is_a: VBO:0400000 {source="https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4142-7153", source="https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5002-8648"} ! Vertebrate breed
relationship: VBO:0300020 http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q258 {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"} ! breed reported in geographic location
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4142-7153
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5520-6597
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9178-3965
property_value: dcterms:source "https://www.fao.org/dad-is" xsd:anyURI
property_value: hasdomesticationstatus VBO:0300007 {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"}
property_value: origindescription "The SA Black ostrich is the result of a selection and breeding programme that started between 1857 and 1864 in the Oudtshoorn district of the Western Cape Province. Breeding material was imported from Libya, Morocco, Syria, Algeria and Tunisia to improve feather quality. By 1911, the SAB feathers were superior to any wild ostrich. The collapse of the feather industry led to diversification and the development of the leather and meat industries. Most of this work was done by the Klein Karoo Agricultural Corporation and the Klein Karoo Agricultural development center. The breed is now classified as Struthio camelus domesticus." xsd:string {source="https://www.fao.org/dad-is"}

